  "number": "1.3",
  "title": "Intercepts",
  "body": " Intercepts  Interpreting the Intercepts  Copper is the third most widely used metal, behind iron and aluminum. In 2007 the US Geological Survey estimated that there are 490 million tons of usable copper reserves left in the world. The global consumption of copper in 2007 was 17.8 million tons per year.   Complete the table of values.    Year  2008  2010  2015  2020             Supposing that we continue to consume copper at the same annual rate, write an equation for the amount of copper, , in millions of tons, remaining years after 2007.  Graph the equation.  Grid [0,30]x[0,500]  Use algebra to find the coordinates of each intercept of the graph.           Explain what the intercepts tell us about copper consumption.  The -intercept is . It tells us:  The -intercept is . It tells us:   General Form for a Linear Equation,   The owner of a gas station has $19,200 to spend on unleaded gas this month. Regular unleaded costs him $2.40 per gallon, and premium unleaded costs $3.20 per gallon.   Write algebraic expressions to answer the questions:  How much do gallons of regular cost?  How much do gallons of premium cost?  Write an equation that relates the amount of regular unleaded gasoline, , and the amount of premium unleaded, , that the owner can buy if he spends $19,200.  Find the intercepts and use the intercept method to sketch the graph.            Grid [0,10]x[0,10]  Explain what the intercepts tell us about the amount of gasoline.  The -intercept is . It tells us:  The -intercept is . It tells us:  If the gas station owner would like to buy 5000 gallons of regular gasoline, how many gallons of premium can he buy with his budget ot $19,200? Label this point on the graph.   What is the meaning of the point on the graph?   Checkpoint   In Activity 1, how did you use algebra to find the intercepts of the graph?  In Activity 1, which intercept gives the starting value of ?  The equation you wrote in Activity 2 was different in form from the equation you wrote in Activity 1. How?  In Activity 2, what did the variables and represent?   "
