  "body": " Intercepts, Solutions, and Factors  A Typical Quadratic Graph   Verify that is a quadratic equation. (Hint: Expand the right side.)  Graph with technology. If you are using a graphing calculator, use the \"friendly\" window Sketch the graph on the grid.  Grid [-4,6]x[-12,8]  Use the Trace feature to locate the -intercepts of the graph.   Solve the equation  Explain how your answers to parts (c) and part (d) are related.    X-Intercepts    10x10 Grid  Use technology to graph the three equations below in the standard window, and sketch the graphs on the grid. What do you notice about the -intercepts?        Multiplying a quadratic expression by a constant does not change the -intercepts of the graph.  Solve by factoring .   Graph on your calculator, using the ZInteger setting.  Locate the -intercepts of the graph.  Use the -intercepts to write the quadratic expression in factored form. (Do not try to factor the expression!)    A Quadratic Model  A rancher has 360 yards of fence to enclose a rectangular pasture. If she uses a riverbank to border one side of the pasture, she can enclose 16,000 square yards of land. What will the dimensions of the pasture be then?  We'll solve this problem in three different ways. First, make a sketch of the pasture and the river.   Method 1: Using a Table of Values  Notice that you only have 360 yards of fence. So if you decide how wide the pasture is going to be, the length of the pasture is determined.   Use the perimeter of the pasture, 360 yards, to write a formula for the length of the pasture in terms of its width. (Be careful: remember that one side of the pasture does not need any fence!)  Length =  Make a table that shows the areas of pastures of various widths.    Width  Length  Area  10  340  3400                     Continue the table until you find the pasture whose area is 16,000 square yards.  Dimensions of pasture:   Method 2: Using a Graph  On your sketch of the pasture in part (1), label the width of the pasture as .   Write an expression for the length of the pasture if its width is . (Hint: How did you compute the length of the pasture in Method 1?  Length =  Write an expression for the area of the pasture if its width is .  Area =  Use technology to graph the equation for . If you use a calculator, set the window at Then copy the graph onto the grid.  Grid [0,200]x[0,20]   On the graph, find the pasture of area 16,000 square yards. Label the correct point on your graph.  Dimensions of pasture:   Method 3: Using an Equation   Refer to steps (a) and (b) of Method 2, and write an equation for the area of the pasture in terms of its width .  Area =   Substitute and solve your equation algebraically.  Dimensions of pasture:  Which of the three methods do you prefer? Why?     Checkpoint   Delbert says that the solutions of the equation are and . Do you agree? Why or why not?  If the perimeter of a rectangle is 56 inches, and its width is inches, write an expression for its length.  How can you use a graph to factor a quadratic expression?  In Method 2 of Activity 3, what did the two variables on the graph represent? What happened to the second variable as you increased the first variable?   "
