  "title": "Quadratic Formula",
  "body": " Quadratic Formula  A Quadratic Model: Free-Fall  A skydiver jumps out of an airplane at 11,000 feet. While she is in free-fall, her altitude in feet seconds after jumping is given by   Use technology to complete the table of values. (You can use the Table feature of your technology tool.)     0  5  10  15  20  25  30           Graph the equation.  Grid [0,30]x[0,12]   Write and solve an equation to answer the question: If the skydiver must open her parachute at an altitude of 1000 feet, how long can she free-fall?  Locate the corresponding point on your graph.    Area and Perimeter  A dog trainer has 100 meters of chain link fence. She wants to enclose a total area of 250 square meters in three pens of equal size, as shown at right. We will find the length and width of each pen.  3 adjacent dog pens, each L wide and W high   Let and represent the length and width of each pen. Label the figure. (How many 's and 's do you have?)  Write an equation in and about the amount of chain link fence. (Hint: Is this an area or a perimeter?)  Solve your equation for in terms of .  Write an equation in terms of and for the total area enclosed.  Substitute your expression for from part (c) into your equation from part (d).   Solve your equation from part (d). (There are two solutions!)  Find the dimensions of each pen. Round your answers to hundredths. (\"Dimensions\" means length and width. There are two different solutions to the problem.)     Summary: Solving Quadratic Equations   List four algebraic methods for solving quadratic equations.        Which method(s) work on any quadratic equation?  Which method is fastest if one side of the equation is a perfect square?  For each equation, name the easiest method for solving, then solve.           Deriving the Quadratic Formula   Use completing the square to solve the equation for in terms of and . Follow the steps below:  Move the constant term to the right side  Complete the square on the left side.  Write the left side as a perfect square; simplify the right side.  Extract roots.  Use completing the square to solve for in terms of , and . Follow the steps below:  Move the constant term to the right side; divide both sides by the lead coefficient.  Complete the square on the left side.  Write the left side as a perfect square; simplify the right side.  Extract roots.    Checkpoint   In Activity 1c, what value did you substitute for ? Why? When is ?  In Activity 2, did you solve an equation about area or about perimeter to find the values of ? How did you use the other quantity in your solution?  In Activity 2f, you found two solutions to the equation. Are these values the dimensions of each pen?  In Activity 4, what was different about the procedure in part (a) from the procedure in part (b)?   "
